# Accessing Application Parameters from a Service in Symfony

When building a Symfony application, you often find yourself needing to read configuration values defined in `app/config` or your custom bundles. In controllers, this is straightforward: you inject the container and call `$this->container->getParameter('my_param')`. Services, however, operate differently by design. They should not extend `Symfony\Bundle\FrameworkBundle\Controller\Controller`, nor should they typically hold direct references to the service container itself.

To access parameters from a service, you have two primary architectural approaches: defining them directly in the service configuration or injecting the parameter via the container during construction. The first approach involves mapping parameters explicitly within your service definition file. In older Symfony versions using YAML, this looks like specifying `parameters` under the service block:

```yaml
# src/Me/MyBundle/Service/my_service/service.yml
services:
    my.service:
        class: My\Bundle\MyBundle\Service\MyService
        parameters:
            my_param1: %my_param1%
            my_param2: %my_param2%
```

In this setup, `%my_param1%` refers to a key defined in your `parameters.yml` (or `config/packages/parameters.yaml` in modern setups). This method is simple and keeps the service constructor clean. You can then access these values directly as class properties or via public methods. However, this approach tightly couples your service definition to specific configuration keys, which can make refactoring harder if parameter names change frequently.

A more flexible and scalable pattern is to pass parameters into the service constructor. This allows you to inject dependencies explicitly, adhering to dependency injection principles. You define a class property or constructor argument that expects the parameter value:

```php
class MyService
{
    private $myParam;

    public function __construct($myParam)
    {
        $this->myParam = $myParam;
    }

    public function doSomething()
    {
        // Use $this->myParam here
    }
}
```

Then, in your service configuration, you tell Symfony to provide the parameter value when instantiating the class:

```yaml
services:
    my.service:
        class: My\Bundle\MyBundle\Service\MyService
        arguments: [ '@parameters.my_param1' ]
```

This technique uses `@parameters.my_param1` (or `kernel.parameters.my_param1` in newer Symfony versions) to resolve the configuration value at runtime. It keeps your service logic decoupled from direct access to the container and makes testing easier, as you can mock or inject different values during unit tests.

One common pitfall is trying to access the service container directly inside a service to retrieve parameters:

```php
// Avoid this in services unless absolutely necessary
public function getParam()
{
    return $this->container->getParameter('my_param'); // Not recommended
}
```

This breaks the single responsibility principle and makes your service harder to test. Instead, prefer injecting what you need directly into the constructor.

If you're working on a larger project or integrating with frameworks like Laravel (as covered in resources such as [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com)), similar principles applyâinject dependencies rather than reaching into global configuration. Always ask: can this value be passed in cleanly, or does it require container magic? When in doubt, favor constructor injection for clarity and maintainability.
